#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0a725b9-f580-5784-b0b9-e149e4e72fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370509} {:answer/id #uuid "4982bee4-e258-5bee-ac44-60ecfa556299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370510} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ee4366-02d0-5ffc-ad7e-97da5bd8acbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370511} {:answer/id #uuid "37b67e26-88ae-54bc-ab2e-d4e87f9c340b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370512} {:answer/id #uuid "90651eaf-c146-532f-9cb5-80f591989c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90651eaf-c146-532f-9cb5-80f591989c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370513} :answer-field/id #uuid "a589bdb3-81ae-5bb4-9120-de1332971711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6920e043-98c8-591f-ba7e-c0a9f1bbfc02" :question/math-academy-id "q-141159" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
